          text: "    geo: {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      lat: 13.2204,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      lng: 75.2533,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      elevation: \"1,892m\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      region: \"Chikkamagaluru\""
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    intro: \"Trekking Kudremukh under heavy South-West monsoon rain isn\'t just a leisure walk. It\'s an intense exercise in weight balancing, trail-reading, and navigating dense montane grasslands where streams form in minutes.\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    content: `### The Logistics of the Trail"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "Standing at **1,892 meters**, Kudremukh (meaning \'Horse Face\' in Kannada) is a massive shola grassland ecosystem. Trekking through it during the heart of the monsoon requires precise preparation:"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "- **Rain Protection:** Heavy-duty canvas packs fitted with internal dry lining. "
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "- **Footwear:** Deep-lugged trail shoes to counter slippery red laterite clay."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "- **Leech Defence:** High-concentration saltwater spray and tight gaiters."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "---"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "### The Terrain Review"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "Our route followed the North-Ridge, crossing three distinct marsh zones before reaching the final steep slope. Mist was so dense that horizontal visibility dropped to less than **5 meters**."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "| Sector | Dist (km) | Elevation Delta | Ground Status |"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "| **Stage 1: Rainforest Canopy** | 3.5 | +250m | Slippery roots, deep humus |"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "| **Stage 2: Shola Grasslands** | 4.0 | +400m | Exposed ridges, gale-force winds |"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "| **Stage 3: The Horse Ridge** | 1.5 | +300m | Red clay, 45-degree granite slabs |"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "### The System Behind Sholas"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "What looks like a scenic green range is an outstanding hydrologic engine. The dense roots of the Shola trees act as a natural sponge, absorbing rain water and slowly releasing it throughout the dry winter months to feed the rivers **Tunga** and **Bhadra**. It is a fully regulated fluid control system, maintaining the water balance of Southern India without a single valve or electronic sensor.`"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
