        lines {
          text: "    },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    intro: \"The Chennakeshava temple is not merely a marvel of devotion; it is a phenomenal demonstration of modular interlocking masonry, tight physical tolerances, and dry-join stone architecture that has survived 900 monsoon cycles.\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    content: `### The Modular Stone Assembly"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "In modern programming, we strive to build \'highly cohesive, loosely coupled\' modules. In the 12th century, Hoysala chief architect **Jakanachari** and his guild utilized identical systems principles when designing Belur\'s main sanctum."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "Instead of binding the dark soapstone (chloritic schist) blocks with mortar, the craftsmen used a sophisticated **Dry Interlocking Joinery**:"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "1. **Mortise and Tenon Joints:** Hand-carved stone pockets where corresponding rock pegs fit with millimeter precisions."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "2. **Groove Slides:** The long bands of reliefs wrapping around the outer walls slide into a backing stone track, resisting shear stress from minor seismic events."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "3. **Weight Distribution:** The towering domes rely entirely on friction and self-centering gravity arcs. Each stone presses into its neighbor, transforming tension into structural stability."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "---"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "### Systems Observation"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "Observe the *Madanika* bracket figures. Each sculpture is a standalone slab of stone, yet they lock into sockets carved under the overhang eaves. This allows easy preservation: if a single block is damaged, it can be decoupled from its matching slot and updated without affecting the load bearing ceiling. "
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "This is the ultimate physically manifested microservices framework!`"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    id: \"travel-hampi\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    type: \"travel\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    title: \"Hampi: The Logic of Hydraulic Flows and Interlocking Ruins\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    subtitle: \"Wandering through the ruins of Vijayanagara, auditing the massive aqueducts and gravity-fed water channels.\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    date: \"2026-01-22\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    readingTime: \"6 min read\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    category: \"Hydrology\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    tags: [\"Aqueducts\", \"Hampi\", \"Geomorphology\"],"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    location: \"Hampi, Karnataka\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    geo: {"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      lat: 15.3353,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      lng: 76.4594,"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      elevation: \"420m\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "      region: \"Vijayanagara\""
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    },"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    intro: \"Hampi\342\200\231s rocky landscape of giant granite boulders is famous for ancient ruins. But underneath the dramatic temples lies a flawless, sprawling hydraulic network of reservoirs, siphon tubes, and aqueduct networks.\","
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "    content: `### Engineering a City on Rocks"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "At its peak in the 15th century, Hampi housed over **500,000 residents**. In a semi-arid zone with rugged topography, bringing water to every public bath, private courtyard, and banana orchard was a critical systems-scaling problem."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "Rather than hauling water manually from the **Tungabhadra River**, Vijayanagara. designers built a gravity-led grid:"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "- **Earthen Main Canal:** Elevated channels brought water from upstream dams directly to the city perimeter."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "- **Intricate Stone Aqueducts:** Perfectly graded stone channels supported by sturdy columns routed the water above street levels."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "- **Stepped Wells (Pushkarani):** Large tanks served as settling basins, executing natural particulate filtration as water seeped through layered levels."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "---"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "### The Siphon Phenomenon"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "One of Hampi\'s hidden engineering marvels is the use of **inverted siphons**. To route water across a steep valley gorge without spilling, builders ran sealed terracotta channels down into the depression and up the other flank, exploiting pressure differentials to force water upward against gravity."
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "It is a stunning reminder that robust, distributed networks succeed when they leverage natural laws rather than attempting to fight them.`"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "  }"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          text: "];"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
        lines {
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
        }
      }
    }
    instruction: "Add Rootent company profile and personal metrics data to data.ts"
    replacement_infos {
