steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1779610352
      nanos: 539819161
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Plan travel section well. Each story will have multiple section and make it easy to navigate. Start with Vietnam, this is first part\n\nThe Plastic Stool Perspective: Decoding the True Style of Northern VietnamThere is a distinct rhythm to Northern Vietnam that you cannot truly appreciate from the window of a tour bus or the comfort of a high-end restaurant. To understand this country, you have to lower your center of gravity. You have to pull up a tiny, brightly colored plastic stool on a crowded sidewalk, inches away from buzzing motorbikes, and let the chaos wash over you.From the frenetic energy of Hanoi\342\200\231s historic quarters to the raw power of an unexpected toofan (typhoon/storm) on C\303\241t B\303\240 Island, here is what it means to experience the unique, unfiltered style of Vietnam.1. The Sidewalk Subculture: Life at Knee-HeightIn Hanoi, the sidewalk is not a walkway\342\200\224it is a communal living room, a kitchen, and a theater all rolled into one.The definitive symbol of Vietnamese street style is the low plastic stool. It is a brilliant, democratic design choice. Whether you are a local office worker in a crisp shirt, an elderly grandmother, or a visiting tech entrepreneur, everyone sits at the exact same level.Sitting so close to the ground fundamentally changes how you interact with a city. It forces you to be present. You look up at the ancient French-colonial architecture, dodge the low-hanging overhead power lines, and watch the endless dance of scooters navigating impossibly narrow alleys. The street dining scene thrives on speed, freshness, and an lack of pretense. If a spot is crowded, you squeeze in, grab a pair of chopsticks, and become part of the pavement architecture.2. A Masterclass in Balance: The FoodVietnamese cuisine is a masterclass in culinary equilibrium. While other Southeast Asian food cultures might hit you with a heavy hammer of spice or rich coconut creams, the local style here is all about clarity, freshness, and contrast.As a vegetarian navigating this landscape, the depth of flavor available is incredible:The Crunch vs. The Soft: A perfect example is the street-side B\303\241nh M\303\254. The crusty, airy baguette fractures into a million shards on the first bite, giving way to soft, rich layers of tofu, pickled daikon, fresh cilantro, and a swipe of chili sauce.The Herb Profile: Every meal is accompanied by a literal mountain of raw greens\342\200\224perilla leaves, mint, fish mint, and coriander. You are expected to tear these up and drop them directly into your hot bowls of vegetarian Pho or noodle salads, creating a brilliant contrast between hot broth and cold, crisp herbs.The Umami Layer: Even without traditional fish sauce, the depth achieved through fermented soy, charred ginger, star anise, and roasted mushrooms creates a rich, clean flavor profile that never feels heavy.3. Ta Hien: The High-Octane Energy of Beer StreetIf the daytime sidewalks are about slow sips of condensed-milk iced coffee, the night belongs to Ta Hien Street\342\200\224affectionately known to travelers worldwide as Beer Street.Walking into Ta Hien after dark is like walking directly into a particle accelerator. The neon signs blaze overhead, music spills out from competing sound systems, and thousands of people are packed shoulder-to-shoulder on those ubiquitous plastic stools.The beverage of choice here is Bia H\306\241i\342\200\224a fresh, light, unpasteurized draft beer brewed daily and delivered in metal kegs each morning. It\342\200\231s incredibly cheap, crisp, and meant to be consumed quickly while snacking on roasted peanuts or fried tofu cubes.But the real draw isn\'t just the drink; it\342\200\231s the sheer kinetic energy. You are sitting so close to the road that when a stray car or delivery scooter tries to push through the pedestrian sea, the entire crowd seamlessly lifts their plastic stools, pulls them back six inches to let the vehicle pass, and drops right back down without missing a beat of their conversation. It is a highly coordinated, beautiful chaos.4. When Nature Reclaims the Coast: The Cat Ba ToofanThe ultimate contrast to Hanoi\342\200\231s calculated chaos happened when transitioning to the coast. C\303\241t B\303\240 Island and the surrounding Lan Ha Bay are famed for their quiet, towering limestone karsts rising out of emerald waters. But nature in Vietnam has a fierce, dramatic side.Experiencing a sudden toofan (typhoon/heavy tropical storm) on the island shifts your perspective instantly.[Hanoi Sidewalks] \342\224\200\342\224\200(Transit)\342\224\200\342\224\200> [Lan Ha Bay Karsts] \342\224\200\342\224\200(The Toofan)\342\224\200\342\224\200> [Raw Island Survival]\n   High Energy                      Serene Beauty                       Fierce Power\nWhen the storm rolls over the Gulf of Tonkin, the serenity of the bay vanishes. The sky turns an ominous slate gray, and the wind begins to howl through the gaps in the ancient limestone cliffs. The ocean transforms from a glass mirror into a churning, violent force.On C\303\241t B\303\240, you see the true resilience of the islanders. While tourists scramble for cover, the locals move with a calm, practiced urgency\342\200\224securing floating fishing villages, tying down boats in the harbor, and pulling down storefront awnings. Watching the sheets of tropical rain hammer the island\'s dramatic topography is a humbling reminder of the sheer power that shapes this landscape. It gives the island a raw, edge-of-the-world feeling that sun-drenched photos simply can\'t capture.The TakeawayThe true style of Vietnam isn\'t found in a museum or a manicured resort. It\342\200\231s found in the gaps between the extremes. It\342\200\231s the ability to find complete comfort on a six-inch plastic stool while a city of millions moves around you, and it\342\200\231s the quiet resilience of an island community standing firm against a coastal storm. It is a country that forces you to look closely, eat deeply, and embrace the unexpected."
    items {
      text: "Plan travel section well. Each story will have multiple section and make it easy to navigate. Start with Vietnam, this is first part\n\nThe Plastic Stool Perspective: Decoding the True Style of Northern VietnamThere is a distinct rhythm to Northern Vietnam that you cannot truly appreciate from the window of a tour bus or the comfort of a high-end restaurant. To understand this country, you have to lower your center of gravity. You have to pull up a tiny, brightly colored plastic stool on a crowded sidewalk, inches away from buzzing motorbikes, and let the chaos wash over you.From the frenetic energy of Hanoi\342\200\231s historic quarters to the raw power of an unexpected toofan (typhoon/storm) on C\303\241t B\303\240 Island, here is what it means to experience the unique, unfiltered style of Vietnam.1. The Sidewalk Subculture: Life at Knee-HeightIn Hanoi, the sidewalk is not a walkway\342\200\224it is a communal living room, a kitchen, and a theater all rolled into one.The definitive symbol of Vietnamese street style is the low plastic stool. It is a brilliant, democratic design choice. Whether you are a local office worker in a crisp shirt, an elderly grandmother, or a visiting tech entrepreneur, everyone sits at the exact same level.Sitting so close to the ground fundamentally changes how you interact with a city. It forces you to be present. You look up at the ancient French-colonial architecture, dodge the low-hanging overhead power lines, and watch the endless dance of scooters navigating impossibly narrow alleys. The street dining scene thrives on speed, freshness, and an lack of pretense. If a spot is crowded, you squeeze in, grab a pair of chopsticks, and become part of the pavement architecture.2. A Masterclass in Balance: The FoodVietnamese cuisine is a masterclass in culinary equilibrium. While other Southeast Asian food cultures might hit you with a heavy hammer of spice or rich coconut creams, the local style here is all about clarity, freshness, and contrast.As a vegetarian navigating this landscape, the depth of flavor available is incredible:The Crunch vs. The Soft: A perfect example is the street-side B\303\241nh M\303\254. The crusty, airy baguette fractures into a million shards on the first bite, giving way to soft, rich layers of tofu, pickled daikon, fresh cilantro, and a swipe of chili sauce.The Herb Profile: Every meal is accompanied by a literal mountain of raw greens\342\200\224perilla leaves, mint, fish mint, and coriander. You are expected to tear these up and drop them directly into your hot bowls of vegetarian Pho or noodle salads, creating a brilliant contrast between hot broth and cold, crisp herbs.The Umami Layer: Even without traditional fish sauce, the depth achieved through fermented soy, charred ginger, star anise, and roasted mushrooms creates a rich, clean flavor profile that never feels heavy.3. Ta Hien: The High-Octane Energy of Beer StreetIf the daytime sidewalks are about slow sips of condensed-milk iced coffee, the night belongs to Ta Hien Street\342\200\224affectionately known to travelers worldwide as Beer Street.Walking into Ta Hien after dark is like walking directly into a particle accelerator. The neon signs blaze overhead, music spills out from competing sound systems, and thousands of people are packed shoulder-to-shoulder on those ubiquitous plastic stools.The beverage of choice here is Bia H\306\241i\342\200\224a fresh, light, unpasteurized draft beer brewed daily and delivered in metal kegs each morning. It\342\200\231s incredibly cheap, crisp, and meant to be consumed quickly while snacking on roasted peanuts or fried tofu cubes.But the real draw isn\'t just the drink; it\342\200\231s the sheer kinetic energy. You are sitting so close to the road that when a stray car or delivery scooter tries to push through the pedestrian sea, the entire crowd seamlessly lifts their plastic stools, pulls them back six inches to let the vehicle pass, and drops right back down without missing a beat of their conversation. It is a highly coordinated, beautiful chaos.4. When Nature Reclaims the Coast: The Cat Ba ToofanThe ultimate contrast to Hanoi\342\200\231s calculated chaos happened when transitioning to the coast. C\303\241t B\303\240 Island and the surrounding Lan Ha Bay are famed for their quiet, towering limestone karsts rising out of emerald waters. But nature in Vietnam has a fierce, dramatic side.Experiencing a sudden toofan (typhoon/heavy tropical storm) on the island shifts your perspective instantly.[Hanoi Sidewalks] \342\224\200\342\224\200(Transit)\342\224\200\342\224\200> [Lan Ha Bay Karsts] \342\224\200\342\224\200(The Toofan)\342\224\200\342\224\200> [Raw Island Survival]\n   High Energy                      Serene Beauty                       Fierce Power\nWhen the storm rolls over the Gulf of Tonkin, the serenity of the bay vanishes. The sky turns an ominous slate gray, and the wind begins to howl through the gaps in the ancient limestone cliffs. The ocean transforms from a glass mirror into a churning, violent force.On C\303\241t B\303\240, you see the true resilience of the islanders. While tourists scramble for cover, the locals move with a calm, practiced urgency\342\200\224securing floating fishing villages, tying down boats in the harbor, and pulling down storefront awnings. Watching the sheets of tropical rain hammer the island\'s dramatic topography is a humbling reminder of the sheer power that shapes this landscape. It gives the island a raw, edge-of-the-world feeling that sun-drenched photos simply can\'t capture.The TakeawayThe true style of Vietnam isn\'t found in a museum or a manicured resort. It\342\200\231s found in the gaps between the extremes. It\342\200\231s the ability to find complete comfort on a six-inch plastic stool while a city of millions moves around you, and it\342\200\231s the quiet resilience of an island community standing firm against a coastal storm. It is a country that forces you to look closely, eat deeply, and embrace the unexpected."
    }
  }
}
